WEBVTT

00:03.760 --> 00:06.600
Okay, moving on to the next use case for the coalesce and Isnull.

00:06.760 --> 00:10.720
We can use them in order to handle the null before doing joins.

00:10.720 --> 00:14.560
This is a little bit advanced use case, but it's very important to understand it.

00:14.560 --> 00:16.600
So let's understand why this is important.

00:16.600 --> 00:19.840
Let's have for example two tables table A and table b.

00:19.880 --> 00:24.320
And in some scenarios we have to go and combine those two tables using the joins.

00:24.320 --> 00:29.840
And now in order to join two tables we have to go and specify the keys between the table A and table

00:29.880 --> 00:31.600
B in order to join on it.

00:31.600 --> 00:34.720
So in this example we have two keys in order to join the tables.

00:34.760 --> 00:36.720
Now here comes the special case.

00:36.960 --> 00:42.960
If those keys don't have any nulls inside it and all the data are filled, then your join can work perfectly

00:42.960 --> 00:44.960
and you will get the expected results.

00:44.960 --> 00:49.400
And now you might have a special case where there are nulls inside the keys.

00:49.400 --> 00:50.640
So they are missing values.

00:50.640 --> 00:56.400
And this is a big problem because in the output you will get unexpected results and some records will

00:56.400 --> 00:57.720
be totally missing.

00:57.720 --> 01:02.900
So in this scenario we have to handle the nulls inside the keys before doing the joins.

01:02.940 --> 01:06.020
Let's have a very simple example in order to understand this behavior.

01:06.060 --> 01:06.340
All right.

01:06.340 --> 01:10.140
So now let's have this very simple example where we have two tables and we want to combine them.

01:10.140 --> 01:13.460
So in the first table we have a year type orders.

01:13.460 --> 01:16.300
And in the second table we have as well year type.

01:16.300 --> 01:17.380
And we have sales.

01:17.380 --> 01:22.340
So now we would like to go and combine those two tables in order to have all informations in one result.

01:22.380 --> 01:27.660
Now we can go of course, and use the inner join between the table one and table two and the keys for

01:27.660 --> 01:28.340
the joins here.

01:28.340 --> 01:33.060
As you can see, we have the year in both of the tables and as well the type.

01:33.060 --> 01:37.260
So we're going to go and use both of those columns as a key for the join.

01:37.260 --> 01:40.060
So let's do it step by step how SQL can execute this.

01:40.060 --> 01:42.460
So we need the year type as the result.

01:42.460 --> 01:45.780
So SQL going to go and take those two columns to the results.

01:46.100 --> 01:48.260
And we need the orders and sales.

01:48.260 --> 01:52.700
So SQL can take as well the orders and the sales from the second table.

01:52.700 --> 01:54.620
So now let's start doing it row by row.

01:54.820 --> 01:57.900
So the first key going to be those two columns.

01:57.900 --> 02:00.940
So we have 2024 and the type A.

02:01.020 --> 02:05.550
So now it's going to start searching for those two informations in the second table.

02:05.550 --> 02:07.910
And as you can see we have here a match right.

02:08.350 --> 02:10.630
So the first row is as well matching.

02:10.750 --> 02:16.430
Since it's inner join it's going to present in the output only the matching rows from left and right.

02:16.710 --> 02:21.230
So in the outputs we're going to get the whole row from the table one.

02:21.230 --> 02:23.950
And we will get the sales from the table two.

02:23.990 --> 02:24.350
All right.

02:24.350 --> 02:25.990
So that's all for the first row.

02:26.030 --> 02:28.550
Now let's move to the second row over here.

02:28.550 --> 02:30.590
So what are the values of the keys.

02:30.910 --> 02:33.710
We have 2024 and the null.

02:33.750 --> 02:38.390
So now if you check the matches on the right side you can see we have a match here.

02:38.430 --> 02:38.710
Right.

02:38.750 --> 02:39.710
It is logical.

02:39.710 --> 02:42.710
So it's as well 2024 and a null.

02:42.710 --> 02:49.230
So everything is matching and we should get it as a result right SQL cannot go and use the equal operator

02:49.350 --> 02:51.470
in order to join tables.

02:51.470 --> 02:56.910
So even though that is logically it makes sense to have it at the output, but still SQL cannot go and

02:56.910 --> 02:58.470
compare the nulls.

02:58.470 --> 03:01.470
That's why this is a problem for this combination.

03:01.590 --> 03:03.650
SQL will not find any matching.

03:03.650 --> 03:08.410
So we will not get any information for the combination of 2024 and NULL.

03:08.450 --> 03:14.450
So for us of course in the business this is missing informations and as well inaccurate results.

03:14.450 --> 03:15.970
So we're going to miss this row.

03:15.970 --> 03:18.290
And this girl going to go and jump to the third row.

03:18.290 --> 03:20.170
So here what are the values of the key.

03:20.330 --> 03:22.290
We have 2025 and a b.

03:22.450 --> 03:25.130
Now it's going to go and search it in the second table.

03:25.130 --> 03:27.290
And it's going to find a match over here.

03:27.290 --> 03:30.410
So in the outputs we're going to get those values.

03:31.410 --> 03:33.010
The order is going to be 50.

03:33.290 --> 03:34.890
The sales 300.

03:34.930 --> 03:36.850
Now it's going to go to the last row.

03:36.850 --> 03:41.610
And we have here again the same problem we have here 2025 and NULL.

03:41.650 --> 03:47.450
And of course if you check the data you will say yes we have a matching over here, but SQL will ignore

03:47.490 --> 03:47.770
it.

03:47.770 --> 03:52.250
So we have exactly the same situation and we will not find it at the results.

03:52.250 --> 03:54.730
So at the output we will get only two rows.

03:54.850 --> 03:59.370
Even though that those two tables are like identical if you compare the keys.

03:59.370 --> 04:04.260
So with that we are losing data at the results and we are providing inaccurate results.

04:04.260 --> 04:08.860
So my friends, if you have nulls inside your keys, what can happen?

04:08.860 --> 04:11.340
You will lose records at the output.

04:11.380 --> 04:17.580
So here it's very important to handle the nulls inside the keys before doing the joins.

04:17.620 --> 04:17.900
All right.

04:17.940 --> 04:22.660
So now in order to fix it we're going to go and use either the coalesce or the Isnull in the join.

04:22.700 --> 04:25.220
So as you can see we are not using the type directly.

04:25.220 --> 04:29.220
We are handling it by replacing the null with an empty string.

04:29.260 --> 04:31.260
It doesn't matter which value you are using.

04:31.460 --> 04:35.500
The main thing is that you have a value and SQL can go and map it.

04:35.500 --> 04:39.620
So you could have it as empty string or a blank or any default value.

04:39.620 --> 04:44.660
But I usually go with the empty string since it's a little bit faster than having any other characters.

04:44.700 --> 04:50.740
So now what's going to happen is we're going to go everywhere and replace those nulls with an empty

04:50.780 --> 04:51.220
string.

04:51.260 --> 04:54.220
So now we don't have any nulls inside our keys.

04:54.340 --> 04:56.140
And let's go and see what can happen.

04:56.140 --> 04:58.420
So we're going to start with the first row again.

04:58.420 --> 05:01.300
Here we have a matching from the right table.

05:01.660 --> 05:05.400
And we're going to see the whole records in the outputs.

05:05.600 --> 05:07.960
So we will get as well the sales as 100.

05:08.000 --> 05:10.680
And now it's going to go to the second row over here.

05:10.920 --> 05:12.760
So this time we don't have a null.

05:12.760 --> 05:15.560
We have 2024 and an empty string.

05:15.600 --> 05:17.440
So now it's going to go and search for match.

05:17.440 --> 05:19.280
And it's going to find it over here.

05:19.280 --> 05:22.400
We have as well 2024 and an empty string.

05:22.400 --> 05:24.600
So now what can happen in the outputs.

05:24.600 --> 05:26.480
We're going to get a 2024.

05:26.880 --> 05:28.640
But here we will get a null.

05:29.320 --> 05:31.000
So we will not get an empty string.

05:31.000 --> 05:32.960
We will get a null over here.

05:32.960 --> 05:36.120
And that's because we are handling the null only on the join.

05:36.120 --> 05:42.120
So as you can see we have here the null type on the join but we don't have it on the select.

05:42.120 --> 05:45.160
So on the select the type is going to be like the original data.

05:45.160 --> 05:46.880
And the original data was null.

05:46.920 --> 05:49.120
We are just handling the null in the joins.

05:49.160 --> 05:53.360
Just in order to let SQL understand how to map and match the data.

05:53.360 --> 05:56.880
So in this example I'm not changing the values in the select.

05:56.880 --> 05:58.680
So that's why we will get the original value.

05:58.680 --> 06:04.040
But the orders we will get it 40 and the sales is going to be 20.

06:04.130 --> 06:05.650
Now moving on to the third row.

06:05.690 --> 06:06.890
I think you already get it.

06:06.890 --> 06:12.010
So it's going to find the match and the sale is going to be 300.

06:12.050 --> 06:12.370
All right.

06:12.410 --> 06:14.050
Now we're going to move to the last one.

06:14.050 --> 06:15.690
And here we have the same scenario.

06:15.930 --> 06:18.690
So we have 2025 and an empty string.

06:18.690 --> 06:20.210
So it's not null anymore.

06:20.370 --> 06:23.210
And it's going to go and search for all those informations.

06:23.210 --> 06:24.770
And it's going to find it over here.

06:24.770 --> 06:30.370
So it's going to take this field over here in the type and null not an empty string.

06:30.370 --> 06:32.850
Because in the select we didn't handle it.

06:32.850 --> 06:38.130
So the order is going to be 60 and the sales is going to be 200.

06:38.130 --> 06:40.610
So as you can see now the result is complete.

06:40.850 --> 06:45.850
We successfully combined both of those tables in one big result using joins.

06:45.850 --> 06:51.970
But as well using the help of the Isnull function in order to have a complete results and not miss any

06:51.970 --> 06:52.530
value.

06:52.530 --> 06:54.570
So my friends, be very careful.

06:54.810 --> 06:57.970
Check always the keys, whether they have nulls or not.

06:58.010 --> 07:03.690
And if you find nulls, go immediately and handle it so you don't lose any records in the results and

07:03.690 --> 07:05.610
you get accurate analysis.
